---
import ServicePage from '../../layouts/ServicePage.astro';
import { pairs } from '../../i18n/ui';

const faq = [
  {
    q: 'Do I have to be spiritual for this to work?',
    a: 'No. The interpretation works just as well if you understand dreams as the work of your subconscious. I match the language to whatever feels right to you.',
  },
  {
    q: 'What makes this different from a psychological interpretation?',
    a: 'I do not work clinically and I do not diagnose. The Aisling Method asks less about what went wrong in your past and more about what your dream is pointing you towards now, and what it is inviting you to do.',
  },
  {
    q: 'Is this fortune telling?',
    a: 'No. I do not predict your future. Dreams sometimes prepare you for what is coming, but as an invitation to pay attention, not as a prophecy.',
  },
  {
    q: 'Which countries do you work in?',
    a: 'Germany, Austria and Switzerland, in English or German. Everything runs in writing, so location makes no difference.',
  },
];
---

<ServicePage
  title="Spiritual Dream Interpretation, Grounded Too"
  description="Spiritual and holistic dream interpretation with the Aisling Method. Kept grounded, without the mystical clutter. 29 € instead of 69 €."
  path="/en/spiritual-dream-interpretation"
  alternates={pairs.spiritual}
  lang="en"
  h1="Spiritual dream interpretation"
  lead="Spiritual work, kept grounded, without the mystical clutter. For people who take their dreams seriously and still want to follow how an interpretation came about."
  serviceName="Spiritual and holistic dream interpretation with the Aisling Method"
  faq={faq}>

  <h2>Between the dictionary and the tea leaves</h2>
  <p>
    Anyone searching for spiritual dream interpretation today usually lands in
    one of two places. Either at free symbol directories that say the same
    thing to everyone. Or at offerings that go so far into the supernatural
    that you can no longer tell where a statement actually came from.
  </p>
  <p>
    There is not much in between. That is exactly where I work.
  </p>
  <p>
    Spiritual here means: I take seriously that your dream wants something.
    That it is not random, and that it comes at a particular moment. Grounded
    means: you can see, step by step, how I arrive at an interpretation. I set
    out what I see in which image and why. None of it is something you have to
    believe.
  </p>

  <h2>Holistic, and what that actually means</h2>
  <p>
    Holistic dream interpretation gets claimed often and explained rarely. For
    me it means that a dream looks at several areas at once:
  </p>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Body.</strong> Dreams often comment on the state of your body before you notice anything yourself</li>
    <li><strong>Direction.</strong> Where you are standing, and which next step would sit right</li>
    <li><strong>Patterns.</strong> What comes back is looking for resolution. Recurring dreams show recurring themes</li>
    <li><strong>Relationships.</strong> When your partner appears in a dream, it is almost always about the relationship itself</li>
    <li><strong>Hidden gifts.</strong> Dreams often know your strengths better than you do</li>
  </ul>

  <h2>One principle that changes a lot</h2>
  <p>
    In the Aisling Method every element of a dream is a part of you. The places,
    the people, the animals, the weather, even your own pace. The unpleasant
    figures too.
  </p>
  <p>
    Which means: a dream never tells you what someone else should change. It
    speaks about your own part in it. That sounds strange at first, but it is
    the freeing part. If everything in your dream is you, then everything that
    can be changed sits with you as well.
  </p>

  <h2>The Aisling Method, briefly</h2>
  <p>
    An Irish tradition that has grown since the 1990s and is used today by
    several hundred certified practitioners worldwide. I have worked with
    dreams since 2011 and learned the method formally from 2023; the
    certifications followed in 2024 and 2026.
  </p>
  <p>
    You can read a full interpretation in this method from beginning to end:
    <a href="/en/example-interpretation">a full interpretation</a>. And why a
    reference book on its own is not enough is
    <a href="/en/blog/what-does-my-dream-mean-dream-dictionary">here</a>.
  </p>

  <h2>Who this suits</h2>
  <p>
    You, if you sense there is more in your dreams but have no appetite for
    generalities. If you want an interpretation that takes you seriously
    without handing you a worldview along with it.
  </p>
  <p>
    I do not push anything on you. You came to understand your dream, not to
    take on a point of view. Take what lands with you, and leave what feels
    foreign.
  </p>

</ServicePage>
